  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 351 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe long term forecast remains on track as a large upper low looks \nto move into the western US by Monday evening. Moisture from what is \nnow Hurricane Simon will be pulled north into the region and \nimpulses ahead of the longwave trough will combine to increase rain \nchances into Tuesday and Wednesday. Some uncertainty remains with \nwhere the highest rain totals will end up over the course of the \nweek. Medium range guidance continues to show the highest chances \n(30-50%) across the Permian basin with lower chances to the east and \nwest of our CWA. A shift in where the moisture ends up may mean more \nrain to the east of west of the Permian Basin. The most organized \nstorms may be capable of producing damaging winds and large hail. \nGiven the tropical origin of the airmass, these storms will be \nefficient rain producers and make localized flash flooding a threat \nthrough Thursday. \n\nDespite the increased rain chances, well above normal \ntemperatures continue through Wednesday before decreasing from \nThursday into next weekend. A cold front approaches and moves \nthrough on Thursday that looks to move temperatures near or just \nbelow normal for mid October. Once the front moves through, highs \non Friday and Saturday only make it into the 60s and 70s for most.\nOvernight lows during that time fall into the 40s and 50s. Rain \nchances decrease late Friday and into Saturday. \n\n-Stickney",
